OVERVIEW. The page continues the previous folio. There Gauss works with two odd primes p and P, each taken with the sign that makes it congruent to 1 mod 4 (e.g. -3, +5, -7, -11, +13). The quantities are:
- f = sign of p, so f = (-1/|p|); F = sign of P.
- e = quadratic character of P mod p (previous page: '4(P,a,a') = f(p-1)-2fe-2'); E = character of p mod P.
- Order-2 cyclotomic numbers such as 4(1,a,a') = f(p-1)-2f-2.
- Four unknowns k, l, m, n counting mixed patterns modulo pP.
The page has three parts: (I) the definition of k, l, m, n; (II) a numerical check with p=5, P=13, pP=65; (III) sign algebra in the ±1 units e, E, f, F.

(I) TOP LEFT. A first notation, pA + A' + Pa + a', is written. Two attempts are struck out. The final scheme is:
- Aa + pA' + Pa' -> k
- Aa + pA' + Pb' -> l
- Aa + pB' + Pa' -> m
- Aa + pB' + Pb' -> n
This matches (1,pA,Pa)=k, (1,pB,Pa)=l, (1,pA,Pb)=m, (1,pB,Pb)=n on the previous page. Capital letters A/B/O mean residue, non-residue or zero for one prime; lower-case a/b/o mean the same for the other prime. Primes denote the class of the neighbouring term in a consecutive or additive pattern. These are numbers of solutions of a linear relation mod pP with prescribed quadratic characters modulo each prime factor. By CRT they factor into products of order-2 cyclotomic numbers.

(II) NUMERICAL CHECK, 5·13 = 65 (my verification).
Each n = 1..65 gets two letters:
- the capital letter gives the character of n mod 13 (A = QR, i.e. n mod 13 in {1,3,4,9,10,12}; B = QNR; O = 0);
- the lower-case letter gives the character mod 5 (a: n ≡ 1,4; b: n ≡ 2,3; o: n ≡ 0).
I checked all 65 entries; all are right except no. 65, which must be 'Oo' (see corrections).
The Aa numbers are 1, 4, 9, 14, 16, 29, 36, 49, 51, 56, 61, 64. Gauss then sorts them by the class of n+1 into nine types (Aa,X):
- (2) Ab: 16, 61 (written 62);
- (3) Ao: 9, 29;
- (5) Bb: 36, 56, and also 1, which Gauss places on row (1);
- (6) Bo: 4, 14, 49;
- (8) Ob: 51;
- (9) Oo: 64;
- (1), (4), (7): empty.
The right-hand column gives general counts:
- (3) (P-5)/4 = 2;
- (6) (P-1)/4 = 3;
- (7) (p-5)/4 = 0;
- (8) (p-1)/4 = 1;
- (9) 1.
Each is a CRT product N_13(A,X)·N_5(a,x) of order-2 cyclotomic numbers. My filled-in steps, for a prime q ≡ 1 mod 4:
- #{x : x, x+1 both QR} = (q-5)/4;
- #{x QR, x+1 QNR} = (q-1)/4;
- #{x QR, x+1 ≡ 0} = 1 (since -1 is a QR).
All the listed counts agree with the data. The full table also gives (1)=0, (2)=2, (4)=0, (5)=3.

(III) RIGHT HALF: sign algebra in e, E, f, F = ±1.
(a) 2eE = 1 + f + F - fF. This is quadratic reciprocity: eE = -1 exactly when f = F = -1. The polynomial is the ±1 encoding of (-1)^{(|p|-1)/2·(|P|-1)/2}. The struck line fF = 1 above it is an abandoned trial.
(b) Multiplying by e (e^2 = 1) gives 2E = e + ef + eF - efF. This is correct.
(c) He tries the symmetric relation e + E + eE = f + F + fF, equivalent to (1+e)(1+E) = (1+f)(1+F). That form is written and struck out lower down. It would say: e = E = 1 if and only if f = F = 1. It is not a consequence of reciprocity. Counterexample from the page's own example: p=5, P=13 gives e = E = -1 and f = F = 1, so LHS = -1 while RHS = 3.
(d) He tries expressions for f in terms of e, E, F:
- 2f = eF + EF + e(...) = eF + EF + F - eEF;
- 2eE + 2fF = ... (partly struck);
- 2fF = e + 1 + eE - E;
- 2 = efF + fF + efEF + f - fEF;
- 2ef = F + 0F + EF.
Each of these encodes a hypothetical case-rule.
(e) To settle such rules he writes the complete 16-row truth table of (e, E, f, F). Some cells are blotted and the first row is marked '+'. He is apparently marking which sign patterns are admissible or satisfy a candidate formula.
(f) He arrives at 2f = eF + F + eEF - EF, rewritten as 2f = (1+e)eF + (e-1)EF = (1+e)F + (e-1)EF. In words: e = +1 gives f = F; e = -1 gives f = -EF. He also writes f = eF + (1/2)(e-1)(EF - eF), meant to be the same rule but not equal to it (see errors).
(g) The bottom box is a case table:
- (e,E) = (+,·): f = F, or f = eF;
- (-,+): f = F, and a formula beginning f = EF - ...;
- (-,-): f = -F, with a garbled formula 'ef + (1/2)(e-1)(EF-ef)'.
This is an attempt to interpolate a case rule by a polynomial in ±1 variables.

PURPOSE. Gauss is evaluating mixed counts k, l, m, n modulo pP in two ways. CRT factorisation brings in e and E (residuacity of each prime modulo the other); a direct count brings in f and F. He seems to be extracting the sign relation that consistency forces, i.e. a counting/cyclotomic route to (or check of) quadratic reciprocity. The next page pursues the same idea with sixteen 4-letter classes and the product (a-P)(A-p) divisible by pP.
